\subsection{Candidate-Regime Dependence of Relative Utility}
\label{sec:results-regimes}

Because ranking utility is defined over competing alternatives, the candidate environment is part of the value being estimated. On the original KuaiLive \emph{room}-ranking task, 10,222 identical user--time--target events yield opposite Memory--Base results when the same fixed raw ranker scores are evaluated under sampled-active and full-active candidates. Among 575 sampled-active rooms, the mean difference is $-0.04120$ (Base $0.60709$, Memory $0.56589$); among full-active rooms, it is $+0.04631$ (Base $0.38571$, Memory $0.43202$). The paired change is $+0.08751$ (95\% CI $[+0.08210,+0.09326]$, 3,000 paired user bootstrap resamples). Neither the interaction histories nor the target event identities change.

The crossed candidate-membership and Base score-normalization reference accounting in Table~\ref{tab:results-factorial} attributes $+0.08827$ of this paired contrast to the membership dimension (95\% CI $[+0.08287,+0.09397]$) and $-0.00076$ to the normalization reference (95\% CI $[-0.00117,-0.00036]$). Membership combines candidate count, identity and rank competition; the contrast does not isolate a causal effect of list length.

