The reversal remains directionally stable across three separately trained KuaiLive checkpoint pairs and nine sampled-candidate draws at sizes 128, 256 and 575 on the same inspected cohort (Supplementary S5 and S8). The retrospective history-state composition remains 4,589 represented, 206 recoverable and 5,427 unavailable events in the paired regimes. The result therefore motivates candidate-conditioned specialist-value supervision, while remaining a retrospective room-ranking diagnostic rather than an independent M3 author-ranking intervention.

The matched M3 author-ranking development analysis tests the decision consequence of this label dependence on the \emph{same} held-out full-active events (Table~\ref{tab:results-m3-dev-regime-transfer}). Policies fitted and calibrated from sampled-candidate labels obtain lower full-active NDCG@10 than policies using full-active supervision: $0.296499$ versus $0.302658$ for SASRec (paired user difference $+0.006159$, 95\% CI $[+0.004835,+0.007543]$) and $0.298123$ versus $0.303093$ for GRU4Rec ($+0.004970$, CI $[+0.003550,+0.006369]$). Realized Memory calls are similar but not identical. A regime-adapted condition uses full-active labels on outer-training users and does not improve on direct full-active supervision. The request-level allocation analysis explains the corresponding quality difference. Although sampled- and full-supervised policies invoke Memory on nearly the same number of requests, they disagree on 10,672 of 50,049 decisions (21.32\%) for SASRec and 11,517 (23.01\%) for GRU4Rec. Among requests added by full-candidate supervision, the observed full-ranking Memory--Base advantage averages 0.133417 and 0.126600, versus 0.076194 and 0.079135 among requests no longer selected. The positive paired utility difference is consequently attributable to allocating a similar invocation budget to higher-value requests, not primarily to changing the overall call fraction (Supplementary S10). These within-development comparisons explain allocation changes and are not an independent held-out evaluation.

