On the prospectively frozen KuaiLive-M3 full-active author TEST, Utility obtains NDCG@10 $0.30287$ with $29.21\%$ realized Memory calls for the primary SASRec fit, versus Base $0.25800$. It exceeds each of the four alternative selector objectives in NDCG while invoking Memory less often (Table~\ref{tab:results-m3-policy}). All policies were calibrated under a maximum $30\%$ DEV call fraction; the observed TEST fractions are reported without retroactive adjustment.

This advantage extends across the three independently trained seeds of both SASRec and GRU4Rec (Table~\ref{tab:results-m3-robustness}; Supplementary S7--S8). In all six fits, Utility has higher TEST NDCG and fewer realized Memory calls than each of the four specified alternative rules. Utility NDCG@10 ranges from $0.30177$ to $0.30400$, its improvement over the corresponding Base from $+0.04237$ to $+0.04515$, and its TEST Memory invocation rate from $27.72\%$ to $29.21\%$. This is joint dominance on the two \emph{observed metrics} for the tested policies, not a claim of identical TEST quotas, lower total serving cost or universal optimality.

For the primary SASRec fit, the predeclared Utility comparisons are against Base ($+0.04486$, user-cluster 95\% CI $[+0.04281,+0.04692]$), Difficulty ($+0.02039$, CI $[+0.01837,+0.02244]$) and Specialist Quality ($+0.02331$, CI $[+0.02118,+0.02551]$). Each passes the within-fit three-comparison Holm correction ($p_{\mathrm{adj}}\approx0.00060$); the remaining fitted-model intervals and tests are in Supplementary S8. The secondary Utility--Uncertainty advantage ranges from $+0.01301$ to $+0.01431$. All six fits use the same 12,533 TEST users, so model-seed consistency is not six independent cohort replications or one globally corrected 18-comparison test family.

