The additional 30,000-target same-period evaluation tests the consequence of \emph{candidate-aligned utility supervision} using frozen policies and previously unscored target event IDs. Under the identical full-active $F$ candidate universe, F-supervised gates exceed S-supervised gates by $+0.005391$ NDCG@10 for SASRec (paired 95\% user-cluster CI $[+0.003646,+0.007174]$) and $+0.003585$ for GRU4Rec (CI $[+0.001599,+0.005576]$); both pass the three-contrast Holm correction ($p_{\mathrm{adj}}<0.001$; Table~\ref{tab:results-m3-b4a-supervision}). In the naturally realized requests, the F-supervised policies make \emph{fewer}, not equal, specialist calls: 8,704 versus 9,105 SASRec calls and 8,629 versus 9,168 GRU4Rec calls. Their absolute weighted full-active NDCG values remain below the fixed Always Memory reference ($0.348744$). These findings corroborate the decision relevance of candidate-aligned supervision for the specified frozen policies; the unequal call counts, unequal DEV label information and shared recording interval preclude strict equal-budget or external temporal-transfer claims.

